{"w":2400,"lines":[{"t":"Test 2","x":0.065892010557057568,"w":0.064092267354329435,"y":0.047772216495144293,"h":0.014920682677505792,"c":1},{"w":0.016494844754536948,"h":0.014534883616744709,"x":0.061855670323762456,"y":0.68313953479046485,"c":1,"t":"C"},{"w":0.71571726481119791,"c":1,"t":"What destroyed the civilisation of Easter Island?","x":0.15451464492078135,"y":0.091111632413253929,"h":0.024620790867965092},{"x":0.11134020660710993,"t":"Easter Isiand, or Rapu Nui as it is known locally, is home to several hundred","w":0.76907216389973954,"y":0.12645348809624923,"h":0.017441861012089777,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"ancient human statues - the moai. After this remote Pacific island was settled by","x":0.11340207172510854,"w":0.81237111409505214,"h":0.016147974163241319,"y":0.14533176755804367},{"y":0.1641221371808349,"w":0.81030929565429677,"h":0.016147974163241319,"c":1,"x":0.11134019741511954,"t":"the Polynesians, it remained isolated for centuries. All the energy and resources"},{"w":0.81867986043294272,"x":0.11127554354640097,"y":0.17965019422594153,"h":0.019828122150736927,"t":"that went into the moai - some of which are ten metres tall and weigh over 7,000","c":1},{"w":0.82268040974934897,"h":0.017615971814445075,"c":1,"y":0.20052847930739559,"t":"kilos - came from the istand itself. Yet when Dutch explorers landed in 1722, they","x":0.11134020742472577},{"x":0.11134019376876893,"y":0.2194767439101899,"w":0.82680414835611982,"t":"met a Stone Age culture. The moai were carved with stone tools, then transported","h":0.017458076835448644,"c":1},{"y":0.23837209274729854,"c":1,"x":0.11134021341792352,"t":"for many kilometres, without the use of animals or wheels, to massive stone","w":0.76701029459635417,"h":0.017441861012089888},{"c":1,"t":"platforms. The identity of the moai builders was in doubt until well into the twentieth","x":0.11134020366320556,"y":0.25572519094016899,"h":0.017615971814445186,"w":0.83917526245117191},{"y":0.27470930204951449,"t":"century. Thor Heyerdahl, the Norwegian ethnographer and adventurer, thought the","x":0.11134019874696605,"w":0.83298970540364592,"h":0.017441861012089777,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"statues had been created by pre-Inca peoples from Peru. Bestselling Swiss author","w":0.83711339314778632,"y":0.29359953016000007,"h":0.015993492923900066,"x":0.11134021053344451},{"h":0.017615971814445075,"x":0.11340205309628799,"w":0.81030929565429688,"y":0.31238989990034738,"c":1,"t":"Erich von Daniken believed they were built by stranded extraterrestrials. Modern"},{"t":"science - linguistic, archaeological and genetic evidence - has definitively proved","h":0.017441861012089777,"c":1,"y":0.32994186018938765,"x":0.11134019376273462,"w":0.82680414835611982},{"w":0.777319590250651,"h":0.017615971814445075,"t":"the moai builders were Polynesians, but not how they moved their creations.","c":1,"x":0.10927834909560848,"y":0.34879624190988623},{"t":"Local folklore maintains that the statues walked, while researchers have tended to","x":0.11134020702718933,"w":0.83092783610026033,"y":0.36627906937497201,"h":0.018923514741907566,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.017441861012089777,"y":0.3851744183268685,"w":0.76907216389973976,"x":0.11134020667081994,"t":"assume the ancestors dragged the statues somehow, using ropes and logs."},{"w":0.80412368774414045,"h":0.017441861012089666,"y":0.41424418577039024,"t":"When the Europeans arrived, Rapa Nui was grassland, with only a few scrawny","c":1,"x":0.11340207353285688},{"t":"trees. In the 1970s and 1980s, though, researchers found pollen preserved in","h":0.017615971814445075,"y":0.43305930724582109,"x":0.1113402097604874,"w":0.78350514729817711,"c":1},{"t":"lake sediments, which proved the island had been covered in lush palm forests for","h":0.015988371754420472,"x":0.1092783616837039,"y":0.45203488388821289,"w":0.83505152384440107,"c":1},{"t":"thousands of years. Only after the Polynesians arrived did those forests disappear.","w":0.83711339314778643,"h":0.017452956225965877,"c":1,"x":0.11134021052999259,"y":0.46946564869532947},{"y":0.48825601887928438,"h":0.017615971814445186,"c":1,"x":0.11134021106413153,"t":"US scientist Jared Diamond believes that the Rapanui people - descendants of","w":0.80618555704752604},{"x":0.11340205361385756,"w":0.77938145955403648,"t":"Polynesian settlers - wrecked their own environment. They had unfortunately","y":0.50726744202742213,"h":0.015988371754420583,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.020230273674042509,"x":0.11128220110912196,"w":0.7959960428873698,"t":"settled on an extremely fragile island - dry, cool, and too remote to be properly","y":0.52445418949529177},{"c":1,"h":0.015996053228641505,"x":0.10927834352904872,"t":"fertilised by windblown volcanic ash. When the islanders cleared the forests for","y":0.54360465132869018,"w":0.8020618693033853},{"x":0.10927834857738512,"w":0.80824742635091129,"h":0.017441861012089777,"y":0.56249999972428288,"c":1,"t":"firewood and farming, the forests didn't grow back. As trees became scarce and"},{"x":0.10923384528812477,"t":"they could no longer construct wooden canoes for fishing, they ate birds. Soil","y":0.57991444668773706,"h":0.020035356755685019,"w":0.78155314127604181,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"erosion decreased their crop yields. Before Europeans arrived, the Rapanui had","w":0.81030929565429677,"y":0.59883720902445636,"h":0.017441861012089777,"x":0.10927834168173613},{"h":0.018895349149765539,"y":0.61773255798027638,"c":1,"x":0.10927834230207377,"w":0.77938145955403648,"t":"descended into civil war and cannibalism, he maintains. The collapse of their"},{"c":1,"x":0.10927835268061036,"y":0.63505578408644126,"t":"isolated civilisation, Diamond writes, is a 'worst-case scenario for what may lie","w":0.79175257364908858,"h":0.019083969465648831},{"c":1,"w":0.31134020487467451,"x":0.10927835395441529,"y":0.65406976755395196,"t":"ahead of us in our own future'.","h":0.017441859892096345},{"w":0.79381444295247405,"h":0.017441861012089777,"c":1,"x":0.10927834540792361,"y":0.68313953460744781,"t":"The moai, he thinks, accelerated the self-destruction. Diamond interprets them"},{"x":0.1092783449241288,"w":0.82474227905273434,"h":0.017615971814445075,"t":"as power displays by rival chieftains who, trapped on a remote little island, lacked","y":0.70199647663359332,"c":1},{"x":0.10927835028531756,"w":0.8144329833984375,"c":1,"y":0.71947674425453323,"t":"other ways of asserting their dominance. They competed by building ever bigger","h":0.017458076835448644},{"w":0.79793813069661468,"c":1,"t":"figures. Diamond thinks they laid the moai on wooden sledges, hauled over log","x":0.10927835713943172,"h":0.017441861012089777,"y":0.73837209274553561},{"w":0.8144329833984375,"t":"rails, but that required both a lot of wood and a lot of people. To feed the people,","h":0.017615971814445075,"x":0.10721649786070828,"c":1,"y":0.75719318826653814},{"h":0.020098551259066011,"c":1,"w":0.82698745727539069,"x":0.10919924407371165,"y":0.77365479418993377,"t":"even more land had to be cleared. When the wood was gone and civil war began,"},{"x":0.10927835764904179,"t":"the islanders began toppling the moai. By the nineteenth century none were","c":1,"h":0.018900469759248417,"y":0.79359953039137243,"w":0.76701029459635417},{"t":"standing.","y":0.81376740040538909,"h":0.015157505826115747,"x":0.10900013589738615,"c":1,"w":0.097672990163167314},{"t":"46","h":0.014534883616744709,"y":0.95203488370695533,"c":1,"x":0.057731958603621271,"w":0.028865979512532551},{"c":1,"y":0.9533535445516701,"x":0.3520980615377719,"w":0.29167673746744793,"h":0.023628416302199917,"t":"leltsresources.org"}],"h":3406}